"""Independent teaching answers for the 121 BC Science Chapter 4 entries.

IDs follow content/bc-newtons-laws-questions.json. Qualitative investigation
answers describe expected observations; numerical results require measured data.
"""
from html import escape
from bc_kinematics_answers import paragraphs as p, parts, table, graph

ANSWERS = {}
def answer(n, body):
    assert f'q-{n}' not in ANSWERS
    ANSWERS[f'q-{n}'] = body

def interaction(title, a, b, vertical=False):
    """Show one interaction pair on two distinct bodies, rather than an FBD."""
    if vertical:
        positions=[(110,155,a),(330,85,b)]
        strokes='<path d="M110 173V219M330 67V21" stroke="#096a70" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M105 212L110 222L115 212M325 28L330 18L335 28" fill="none" stroke="#096a70" stroke-width="3"/>'
        labels='<text x="110" y="244" text-anchor="middle">B pushes A down</text><text x="330" y="15" text-anchor="middle">A pushes B up</text>'
    else:
        positions=[(110,110,a),(330,110,b)]
        strokes='<path d="M110 80H45M330 80H395" stroke="#096a70" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M53 75L43 80L53 85M387 75L397 80L387 85" fill="none" stroke="#096a70" stroke-width="3"/>'
        labels='<text x="110" y="55" text-anchor="middle">B pushes A left</text><text x="330" y="55" text-anchor="middle">A pushes B right</text>'
    boxes=''.join(f'<rect x="{x-70}" y="{y-18}" width="140" height="36" rx="7" fill="#e3eee8" stroke="#096a70"/><text x="{x}" y="{y+4}" text-anchor="middle">{escape(name)}</text>' for x,y,name in positions)
    return f'<figure class="answer-graph"><svg viewBox="0 0 450 270" role="img" aria-label="{escape(title,quote=True)}"><title>{escape(title)}</title>{boxes}{strokes}{labels}</svg><figcaption>{escape(title)}. Only the interaction pair is shown; equal forces act on different bodies.</figcaption></figure>'

answer(1,p('This is physics. The dishes tend to remain at rest because of inertia. A fast, smooth pull lets friction from the cloth act for only a short time, so the impulse and change in the dishes’ momentum are small. They may move a little; inertia does not mean that friction is absent.'))
answer(2,p('A similarly sized rock usually has much greater mass and therefore greater inertia. It is harder to accelerate, so stopping your foot against it can produce a larger force. Its greater rigidity and shorter deformation time also matter; mass alone does not determine the impact force.'))
answer(3,p('Their <strong>inertial mass is unchanged</strong>, assuming their bodies have not lost or gained material. Apparent weightlessness in orbit results from astronauts and station falling together, not from a disappearance of mass or inertia.'))
answer(4,p('If tire-road friction cannot provide the sideways force needed for the turn, the car tends to continue along its current straight-line direction, approximately tangent to the intended curve. Newton’s first law applies to the net force, not to what direction the wheels point.'))
answer(5,p('The description does not match the ordinary inertia effect of braking. A loose suitcase continues forward relative to the slowing bus, so it would move <strong>from the rear toward the front</strong>, rather than fly backward from the front toward a passenger behind it. A different force or prior motion would be needed to produce the stated path.'))
answer(6,p('When the cup accelerates, brakes, or turns, the liquid tends to continue its previous motion. It can move relative to the cup and spill. The lid contains the liquid while contact forces redirect or accelerate it with the cup.'))
answer(7,p('When the toy car stops suddenly, the unattached figure continues forward until another force stops it. This illustrates inertia: stopping the vehicle does not automatically stop the passenger.'))
answer(8,p('A seatbelt exerts a restraining force on the passenger, changing the passenger’s velocity along with the car. It prevents continued forward motion into the interior or out of the vehicle.'))
answer(9,p('The belt restrains the passenger and distributes the stopping force over stronger parts of the body. By allowing a controlled stopping interval, it can reduce the average force for the same change in momentum: F<sub>avg</sub> = Δp/Δt.'))
answer(10,p('As the bag starts moving, the air tends to remain at rest and lags relative to the bag. When the bag stops, the air tends to keep moving and presses or bulges the opposite side. Both observations show that air has mass and resists changes in motion. Describe what your own demonstration actually shows.'))
answer(11,p('On a level air track, after the nudge and while the glider is away from the ends, the net force is <strong>approximately zero</strong>. Weight downward and the air support upward balance; horizontal friction is small. Forces exist even though their resultant is nearly zero.'))
answer(12,p('After the nudge, the glider should travel in a straight line at nearly constant velocity until it reaches an end. A slight speed change can result from residual drag or a track that is not perfectly level.'))
answer(13,p('With approximately zero net force, the glider maintains its velocity rather than requiring a continued push. That is Newton’s first law.'))
answer(14,p('The expected initial path is <strong>a straight line tangent to the circle</strong>, option (c). Once the guiding track stops providing a sideways force, the train retains its instantaneous direction of motion. Later wheel forces and friction can alter its path.'))
answer(15,p('With a slow pull, the upper string carries the weight plus the additional pull: T<sub>upper</sub> ≈ mg + T<sub>lower</sub>, so it normally breaks first. With a sufficiently quick jerk, the mass barely moves initially because of inertia, and the large brief tension in the lower string can break it first. The result depends on the strings and how the pull is applied.'))
answer(16,p('The <strong>slow pull</strong> highlights the weight: the upper string must support mg as well as the pull. The <strong>quick jerk</strong> highlights inertial mass: the hanging object resists rapid acceleration.'))
answer(17,p('After launch, the ideal model includes <strong>gravity downward</strong>. In the real demonstration, air resistance also acts. The launcher exerts no continuing force once contact ends.'))
answer(18,p('At release, the ball already has the cart’s horizontal velocity. With negligible horizontal force it maintains that velocity while gravity changes its vertical motion. If the cart continues at the same velocity, the ball returns to the cannon.'))
answer(19,p('A miss can result from the trigger jerk changing the cart’s speed, rolling friction, an angled launch, or drag on the ball. Newton’s first law still applies: the cart and ball need not experience the same external forces after release.'))
answer(20,p('A sharp jerk gives the roll little time to accelerate, so its rotational inertia resists unwinding and the tension can tear the perforation. A slow pull lets the roll turn and supply more paper.'))
answer(21,ANSWERS['q-1'])
answer(22,p('The 2 kg of apples has <strong>twice the inertia</strong> of 1 kg. For the same net force its acceleration is half as large, because a = F<sub>net</sub>/m.'))
answer(23,p('No. Gravity pulls downward on the pen and the desk provides an upward normal force. These can balance, giving zero net force even though individual forces act.'))
answer(24,p('Balanced forces imply <strong>constant velocity</strong>, which can be zero or nonzero. They do not by themselves prove that the pen is at rest. If it is already at rest, it remains at rest while the forces stay balanced.'))
answer(25,p('The ball initially tends to remain at its original position while the wagon accelerates forward. It therefore appears to move <strong>backward relative to the wagon</strong>. Contact forces can subsequently accelerate it.'))
answer(26,p('When a car is accelerated from behind, the seat pushes the torso forward while the head tends to retain its previous motion. The headrest provides a force to move the head with the torso and limits their relative motion.'))
answer(27,p('No. The puck slows because friction and drag exert a net force opposite its motion. The first law predicts constant velocity only when the net force is zero.'))
answer(28,p('Your body tends to continue forward at its previous velocity while the bus slows. You therefore move forward relative to the bus until the seat, belt, or another contact force slows you.'))
answer(29,p('The intended textbook answer is <strong>zigzag motion</strong>: a massive animal needs a large sideways force to change direction quickly. Inertia helps explain that reasoning, but actual turning ability also depends on speed and available ground forces; mass alone does not establish which route would succeed.'))
answer(30,p('For the described motion to the right, the spool’s acceleration and the <strong>net horizontal force</strong> point right. F<sub>net</sub> = ma. The pull is not necessarily the only horizontal force: friction also contributes when the spool rolls. Use your observations to identify the actual acceleration.'))
answer(31,p('Reversing the winding does not reverse the direction of a pull that is still horizontal to the right. For an ordinary spool with inner winding radius smaller than its outer rolling radius and sufficient grip, either winding can produce acceleration to the right, although torque and acceleration magnitude can differ.','If the pulling angle, slipping, or geometry changes, reassess the observed motion from the total force and torque rather than assuming the applied force alone determines everything.'))
answer(32,p('Treat the stated thrust as the net force: F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (1500)(0.400) = <strong>600 N</strong> in the acceleration direction.'))
answer(33,p('Under the quick check’s assumption that 25 000 N is the net accelerating force, m = F<sub>net</sub>/a = 25 000/2.0 = <strong>1.25 × 10⁴ kg</strong> before rounding, about 1.3 × 10⁴ kg. If this were total upward thrust near Earth, weight would also need to be included.'))
answer(34,p('a = F<sub>net</sub>/m = 50 000/250 000 = <strong>0.20 m/s²</strong>, in the net-force direction.'))
answer(35,p('For a horizontal push on a level floor, N = 45.0 N. Kinetic friction = μ<sub>k</sub>N = (0.333)(45.0) = 14.985 N.','F<sub>net</sub> = 25.0 − 14.985 = 10.015 N. Thus a = 10.015/4.60 = <strong>2.18 m/s²</strong> in the pushing direction. Rounding friction to 15.0 N during the calculation gives the source sample’s 2.17 m/s²; either reflects the same model.'))
answer(36,p('The 20 N value is the object’s weight: m = F<sub>g</sub>/g = 20/9.8 = <strong>2.0 kg</strong> (2.04 kg before rounding).','Air resistance affects acceleration, not the mass calculated from weight. The net downward force is 20 − 6.0 = 14 N; it is not appropriate to divide 14 N by g to find the mass.'))
answer(37,p('If the stationary person distributes their load equally, <strong>each scale reads 450 N</strong>, because N₁ + N₂ = 900 N.','Equal sharing is an assumption. Leaning to one side changes the individual readings; only their sum is determined without additional information.'))
answer(38,p('Assuming the paratrooper accelerates <strong>downward</strong> at 4.5 m/s², choose downward positive: mg − 400 = ma.','m = 400/(9.81 − 4.5) = <strong>75 kg</strong> (75.3 kg before rounding).','The direction of acceleration is not stated. If acceleration is upward at 4.5 m/s² while the person descends and slows, 400 − mg = ma gives <strong>28 kg</strong>. The downward interpretation is the usual intended one.'))
answer(39,parts('F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (5.0)(6.0) = 30 N. Friction = 50 − 30 = <strong>20 N</strong>, opposite the motion.','N = mg = (5.0)(9.81) = 49.05 N. μ<sub>k</sub> = 20/49.05 = <strong>0.41</strong>, dimensionless.'))
answer(40,p('A constant net force on a system of constant mass gives constant acceleration. If the cart starts from rest and moves in the force direction, its speed rises uniformly: <strong>v = at</strong>. More generally v = v₀ + at for the signed velocity.'))
answer(41,p('For fixed total system mass, <strong>a = F<sub>net</sub>/M</strong>, so acceleration is directly proportional to net force. A graph of a versus F should be approximately straight through the origin.','Use your measured slopes and uncertainty to support the claim. No acceleration data are filled into the source table, so no experimental numerical slope can be supplied.')+table(['Applied-force case','Expected acceleration relative to the 2.0 N case'],[('2.0 N','1'),('4.0 N','2'),('6.0 N','3'),('8.0 N','4')]))
answer(42,p('At constant net force, a ∝ 1/M. Doubling, tripling, and quadrupling total mass reduce acceleration to <strong>½, ⅓, and ¼</strong> of its original value.','The a-versus-M graph is a decreasing inverse curve. The a-versus-1/M graph is a straight line through the origin. Compare these model predictions with your measured data.'))
answer(43,p('<strong>Your measured slope is required.</strong> If the horizontal axis is 1/M in kg⁻¹ and a is in m/s², the equation is a = F<sub>net</sub>(1/M), and the slope has units kg·m/s² = N.','The source instead asks for a in cm/s² and mass in “mass units.” If u is the numerical number of mass units and one unit has actual mass M₀ kg, then <strong>a = S(1/u)</strong>, where S = 100F<sub>net</sub>/M₀ in cm/s² per unit of 1/u. Expressing the reciprocal-mass axis in mass-unit⁻¹ gives slope units cm·mass-unit/s². Determine M₀ and the numerical slope from your setup and data.'))
answer(44,p('Possible difficulties include pulley and wheel friction, a track not correctly tilted to compensate for friction, tension differing from the hanging weight while the hanging mass accelerates, timer calibration, tape drag, and uncertainty in dot spacing and total moving mass.','Include the cart, hanging mass, and other relevant moving components in the system mass. Keep total mass fixed when varying the hanging driving weight; repeat runs and fit the velocity-time data rather than relying on a single interval.'))
answer(45,p('F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (5.0)(5.0) = <strong>25 N</strong>.'))
answer(46,parts('a = (7.5 × 10⁴)/(3.0 × 10⁴) = <strong>2.5 m/s²</strong>.','v = v₀ + at = 5000 + (2.5)(25) = <strong>5062.5 m/s before rounding</strong>, about 5.1 × 10³ m/s at the precision of the givens.'))
answer(47,parts('Assuming launch from rest, a<sub>avg</sub> = (60.0 − 0)/1.2 = <strong>50 m/s² upward</strong>.','Average net force = ma = (0.12)(50) = <strong>6.0 N upward</strong>.','If drag is neglected, thrust − weight = net force, so thrust = 6.0 + 1.2 = <strong>7.2 N upward</strong>.'))
answer(48,p('m = F<sub>net</sub>/a = (2.4 × 10³)/(4.0 × 10¹) = <strong>60 kg</strong>.'))
answer(49,parts('F<sub>net</sub> = 3.3 × 10⁷ − 2.9 × 10⁷ = 4.0 × 10⁶ N upward. a = (4.0 × 10⁶)/(3.0 × 10⁶) = <strong>1.3 m/s² upward</strong>.','As fuel is expelled the rocket mass changes. Gravity, thrust, and air resistance can also change during flight, so the ratio F<sub>net</sub>/m need not remain constant.'))
answer(50,p('Choose the initial travel direction positive: a<sub>avg</sub> = (0 − 5.5)/5.0 = −1.1 m/s². Friction magnitude = m|a| = (60.0)(1.1) = 66 N.','μ<sub>rolling</sub> = F<sub>fr</sub>/N = 66/(5.9 × 10²) = <strong>0.11</strong>.'))
answer(51,parts('Stairs: push downward on the step; the step pushes upward on you, helping you rise.','Swimming: push water backward; the water pushes you forward.','Rowing: the oars push water backward; the water pushes the oars and boat forward.','Powered car: the driven tires push the road backward; the road’s forward static friction pushes the car forward.')+interaction('Stairs interaction','A: stairs','B: person',True)+interaction('Swimming interaction','A: water','B: swimmer')+interaction('Rowing interaction','A: water','B: boat/oars')+interaction('Driven tire interaction','A: road','B: tire/car'))
answer(52,ANSWERS['q-51']+p('Either member can be called “action”; the other is “reaction.” They are simultaneous and equal in magnitude, and neither waits for the other.'))
answer(53,p('Your foot pushes backward on the ground. Static friction from the ground pushes forward on your foot, accelerating your body forward. The paired forces act on different bodies. The upward normal force also supports you, but it is the forward component that produces forward acceleration.'))
answer(54,p('The dog pushes the log left; the log pushes the dog right. With negligible external horizontal force, the log moves <strong>left</strong> as the dog moves right. Their total horizontal momentum remains constant; the amount of recoil depends on their masses.'))
answer(55,parts('The compressed spring bumpers exert repelling contact forces on the carts.','The equal-mass carts experience equal force magnitudes and therefore equal acceleration magnitudes, a = F/m, in opposite directions.','Each cart pushes the other with an equal and opposite force over the same interaction interval, illustrating Newton’s third law.'))
answer(56,parts('For the same spring compression, the <em>initial</em> spring force is unchanged; the force on one cart still equals the opposite force on the other. Changing the masses can change the subsequent release timing and force history.','With one cart twice as massive, its acceleration magnitude at a given interaction force is half the other cart’s. The lighter cart moves away faster; from an initially stationary, isolated pair, m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ = 0.'))
answer(57,parts('The two carts still experience equal and opposite forces. With the same initial spring compression the initial force is the same as before, though the later force history may differ.','The much heavier cart has much smaller acceleration and recoil speed; the light cart moves much more. At each instant |a<sub>heavy</sub>|/|a<sub>light</sub>| = m<sub>light</sub>/m<sub>heavy</sub>.'))
answer(58,parts('The car moves forward because the board exerts a forward frictional force on its driven wheels.','The car pushes the lightweight board backward, so the board recoils in the opposite direction. If the car moves right, the board moves left, assuming negligible horizontal drag from the air table.'))
answer(59,p('The wheels exert a backward force on the board, and the board exerts an equal forward force on the wheels. These forces act on different bodies, so each can accelerate in the opposite direction.'))
answer(60,p('For a light string in approximately static equilibrium, <strong>both balances read the same tension T</strong>. The actual reading depends on the applied pull; “20 N” is the balance’s capacity, not the necessarily observed force.','If each student pulls with 10 N, each balance reads 10 N, not 20 N. Record the actual common reading.'))
answer(61,p('The readings remain equal in the light-string, quasi-static model, regardless of which student initiates the pull. A stronger pull raises the common tension. Transient accelerations and non-negligible string or instrument mass can produce small differences during a rapidly changing pull.'))
answer(62,p('Adding a third balance in series should give the same reading T on all three while the system is nearly static. Each connected body exerts an equal and opposite force on its neighbor. Tension does not add merely because more balances are in series.'))
answer(63,p('The driven wheels push the road <strong>backward</strong>. The road’s forward static-friction force on the wheels is the external force that propels the car forward. It must exceed resistance if the car is to accelerate.'))
answer(64,p('Examples: a foot pushes the floor backward and the floor pushes the walker forward; a swimmer pushes water backward and water pushes the swimmer forward; a balloon pushes expelled air backward and that air pushes the balloon forward. Identify both bodies and both forces in each example.'))
answer(65,p('The glove exerts an equal and opposite force on the ball, slowing and stopping it. The ball’s force acts on the glove; the reaction acts on the ball.'))
answer(66,p('You may call the club’s force on the ball the action and the ball’s force on the club the reaction. Reversing those labels is equally valid; both occur simultaneously.')+interaction('Golf club and ball','A: club','B: ball'))
answer(67,p('The ball exerts a reaction force of <strong>100 N</strong> on the foot in the opposite direction.'))
answer(68,p('In the usual light-rope, quasi-static tug-of-war model, the two end forces have <strong>equal magnitudes</strong>. The stronger participant does not create an unequal action-reaction pair.','For a rope with significant mass and acceleration, its two end forces can differ by m<sub>rope</sub>a. Those two end forces are not themselves a third-law pair; each has its own partner on the person at that end.'))
answer(69,p('Both cars experience <strong>the same impact-force magnitude</strong>, in opposite directions, at every instant of the collision. Different masses can produce different accelerations and damage; that does not alter the third-law pair.'))
answer(70,p('The board exerts <strong>2000 N</strong> on the hand in the opposite direction during the stated interaction.'))
answer(71,p('In the idealized scenario, throw a tool <strong>away from the station</strong>. Your force accelerates the tool away, while its equal and opposite force gives you momentum toward the station. With negligible external impulse, the total momentum of you and the tool is conserved.'))
answer(72,parts('The intended choice for the largest impact is <strong>softball at 10 m/s</strong>.','The intended choice for the smallest impact is <strong>feather at 2 m/s</strong>.')+p('These choices use the assumed ordering of object masses and speeds. Actual impact force and injury also depend on stopping time, shape, and material; momentum alone does not determine pain.'))
answer(73,parts('For the most massive listed object at the greatest speed, p = mv and kinetic energy ½mv² are greatest, so stopping it generally requires a larger momentum transfer.','For the least massive listed object at the lowest speed, momentum and kinetic energy are smallest, making the intended impact the least severe under otherwise similar conditions.'))
answer(74,p('p = mv = (100)(10) = <strong>1.0 × 10³ kg·m/s</strong>, in the motorbike’s travel direction.'))
answer(75,p('Convert 200 km/h to 200/3.6 = 55.56 m/s.','m = p/v = (1.1 × 10⁶)/(55.56) = 19 800 kg ≈ <strong>2.0 × 10⁴ kg</strong>.'))
answer(76,p('v = p/m = 0.25/0.01 = <strong>25 m/s</strong>, or 90 km/h, before rounding.','The equation allows this value mathematically. Whether an actual insect of this mass can sustain it requires biological and aerodynamic information not supplied in the question; no impossibility follows from p = mv alone.'))
answer(77,p('Take the player’s initial running direction as positive.')+parts('p<sub>i</sub> = mv = (112)(3.6) = <strong>+403.2 kg·m/s</strong>, about +4.0 × 10² kg·m/s.','J = Δp = p<sub>f</sub> − p<sub>i</sub> = 0 − 403.2 = <strong>−403.2 N·s</strong>, opposite the initial motion.','F<sub>avg</sub> = J/Δt = −403.2/0.80 = −504 N ≈ <strong>−5.0 × 10² N</strong>.','The minus sign means that the force points opposite to the direction defined as positive. It does not mean that the force magnitude is negative.'))
answer(78,p('Assume the sulphur has zero initial horizontal velocity and external horizontal impulse is negligible. Conserve horizontal momentum of car plus sulphur:','(6000)(5.5) + (3000)(0) = (6000 + 3000)v. Thus v = 33 000/9000 = <strong>3.7 m/s</strong> in the original direction. The added load increases moving mass and reduces speed.'))
answer(79,p('For equal masses m, mu + m(0) = (2m)(2.0). Therefore <strong>u = 4.0 m/s</strong> in the direction of the combined gliders.'))
answer(80,p('Initial momentum = (1.0)(1.5) + (4.5)(0) = <strong>1.5 kg·m/s</strong>. With negligible external impulse, this is also the combined final momentum, in the putty’s initial direction.','If a final velocity is desired, v = 1.5/(1.0 + 4.5) ≈ 0.27 m/s; the question asks for momentum.'))
answer(81,p('Let m be the initially moving ball’s mass and assume both balls share the stated final velocity.','m(3.5) = (m + 5.0)(2.5), so (3.5 − 2.5)m = 12.5. Hence <strong>m = 12.5 kg</strong> before rounding, about 13 kg.'))
answer(82,p('75 g = 0.075 kg. p = (0.075)(2.6) = 0.195 kg·m/s ≈ <strong>0.20 kg·m/s</strong>, in the mouse’s direction.'))
answer(83,p('1.0 ms = 1.0 × 10⁻³ s. J = FΔt = (55)(1.0 × 10⁻³) = <strong>0.055 N·s</strong>, in the force direction.'))
answer(84,p('Choose the bullet’s direction positive. For an initially stationary rifle-bullet system with negligible external impulse, 0 = m<sub>b</sub>v<sub>b</sub> + m<sub>r</sub>v<sub>r</sub>.','v<sub>r</sub> = −(0.060)(600)/3.0 = <strong>−12 m/s</strong>, backward.'))
answer(85,p('Include both skateboards: moving mass = 53 + 2.0 = 55 kg; initially stationary mass = 43 + 2.0 = 45 kg.','v = [(55)(1.6) + (45)(0)]/(55 + 45) = <strong>0.88 m/s</strong> in the first skateboarder’s initial direction, assuming they move together afterward.'))
answer(86,p('J = Δp = m(v<sub>f</sub> − v<sub>i</sub>) = (10.0)(25.5 − 12.6) = <strong>129 N·s</strong>.','F<sub>avg</sub> = J/Δt = 129/5.00 = <strong>25.8 N</strong>. Both point in the direction of increasing velocity.'))
answer(87,p('Choose the first car’s direction positive. Initial total momentum = (1500)(44) + (1000)(−22) = 44 000 kg·m/s.','After sticking together, v = 44 000/(1500 + 1000) = <strong>+17.6 m/s</strong> before rounding, about 18 m/s in the first car’s original direction.')+table(['Object','Mass (kg)','Initial velocity (m/s)','Initial momentum (kg·m/s)'],[('Car 1',1500,44,66000),('Car 2',1000,-22,-22000),('Combined',2500,'17.6 after collision',44000)]))
answer(88,parts('145 g = 0.145 kg. J = m(v<sub>f</sub> − v<sub>i</sub>) = (0.145)(−50.0 − 40.0) = −13.05 N·s ≈ <strong>−13.1 N·s</strong>.','1.00 ms = 0.00100 s. F<sub>avg</sub> = −13.05/0.00100 = <strong>−1.31 × 10⁴ N</strong>. The force on the ball is opposite its initial positive velocity.'))
answer(89,p('When the car stops, an unrestrained person tends to continue at the car’s former velocity until another force acts on them. A seatbelt supplies a controlled restraining force and prevents that continued motion into the windshield or other interior surfaces.'))
answer(90,p('In an inertial frame, F<sub>net</sub> = ma. If F<sub>net</sub> = 0 and m is nonzero, then a = 0, so velocity remains constant. This includes remaining at rest and continuing in straight-line uniform motion: Newton’s first law.'))
answer(91,p('F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (2.0 × 10³)(1.5) = <strong>3.0 × 10³ N</strong>.'))
answer(92,p('a = F<sub>net</sub>/m = (1.16 × 10²)/(5.8 × 10³) = <strong>0.020 m/s²</strong>.'))
answer(93,p('m = F<sub>net</sub>/a = (2.0 × 10²)/0.40 = <strong>5.0 × 10² kg</strong>.'))
answer(94,p('F<sub>net</sub> = 12.8 − 2.8 = 10.0 N. a = 10.0/5.0 = <strong>2.0 m/s²</strong> in the applied-force direction.'))
answer(95,p('<strong>No</strong>, in the stated ideal, steady pulling case. The rope tension is 500 N, below its 750 N limit. Opposing 500 N pulls do not produce 1000 N of tension; they balance the rope’s net force while keeping 500 N of internal tension.'))
answer(96,p('When A exerts a force on B, B simultaneously exerts an equal-magnitude, opposite-direction force on A. The forces act on different bodies.','Example: a balloon pushes gas backward as it escapes, and the gas pushes the balloon forward.')+interaction('Balloon and expelled air','A: expelled air','B: balloon'))
answer(97,p('The teams experience equal rope tension in the light-rope model, but each team also interacts with the ground. The team that can maintain greater effective traction can produce a net external force on the combined teams-and-rope system, moving it toward that team. The other team’s feet may slip. Newton’s third law is obeyed throughout.'))
answer(98,parts('Momentum is the vector quantity <strong>p = mv</strong>, with units kg·m/s.','Total momentum is conserved when the system’s net external impulse is zero. This allows predictions about collisions, recoil, and explosions even when the internal forces are complicated.'))
answer(99,parts('Impulse is the time integral of force; for an average force, <strong>J = F<sub>avg</sub>Δt = Δp</strong>. Its unit is N·s, equivalent to kg·m/s.','46 g = 0.046 kg. J = (0.046)(60.0 − 0) = <strong>2.76 N·s</strong> before rounding, about 2.8 N·s.','0.50 ms = 0.00050 s. F<sub>avg</sub> = 2.76/0.00050 = <strong>5.5 × 10³ N</strong>, in the ball’s acceleration direction.'))
answer(100,p('The rifle pushes the bullet forward and the bullet pushes the rifle backward. If loosely supported, the rifle recoils before striking the shoulder; stopping it over a short time produces a large average contact force. Firm contact couples its motion to the shooter earlier.'))
answer(101,p('For zero initial momentum, v<sub>rifle</sub> = −m<sub>bullet</sub>v<sub>bullet</sub>/m<sub>rifle</sub> = −(0.050)(300)/4.2 = <strong>−3.6 m/s</strong>, opposite the bullet’s direction.'))
answer(102,p('Both initial velocities point the same way: v = [(0.250)(5.0) + (0.300)(2.0)]/(0.250 + 0.300) = 1.85/0.550 = <strong>3.4 m/s</strong> in their original direction.'))
answer(103,p('For identical masses m: m(6.0) + m(0) = (2m)v. Thus <strong>v = 3.0 m/s</strong> in the moving car’s original direction.'))
answer(104,p('<strong>Inertial mass</strong> measures resistance to acceleration: m<sub>i</sub> = F<sub>net</sub>/a. <strong>Gravitational mass</strong> measures coupling to gravity, for example F<sub>g</sub> = m<sub>g</sub>g. Their measured equality allows a balance based on gravity to determine inertial mass. Both are reported in kilograms.'))
answer(105,p('A full tanker has more mass and therefore more inertia. With comparable available forces, it is harder to <strong>start or speed up</strong>, <strong>stop or slow down</strong>, and <strong>turn</strong>. Changing velocity requires a greater impulse for the same velocity change; a given force produces less acceleration.'))
answer(106,ANSWERS['q-26'])
answer(107,p('Without a headrest, a rear impact can accelerate the torso through the seat while the head initially retains its previous motion. The resulting relative movement bends the neck. A headrest can exert a force on the head so that it moves more nearly with the torso.'))
answer(108,p('Ice provides relatively small opposing friction, so the puck’s horizontal net force and deceleration are small. It continues moving for a long distance; in the ideal zero-net-force limit it would maintain constant velocity.'))
answer(109,p('The inertia argument applies to pets as well as people: when a car stops or turns suddenly, an unrestrained animal tends to retain its previous motion. An appropriate restraint provides a force to change that motion with the car instead of allowing uncontrolled movement through the cabin.'))
answer(110,p('From F<sub>net</sub> = ma, [F] = [m][a] = <strong>kg × m/s² = kg·m/s²</strong>. One newton is defined as 1 kg·m/s².'))
answer(111,p('Total mass = 35 + 20 = 55 kg. F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (55)(1.0) = <strong>55 N</strong>.'))
answer(112,p('The stated 270 N is weight: m = 270/9.81 = 27.52 kg. F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (27.52)(0.7) = 19.27 N ≈ <strong>19 N</strong> in the acceleration direction. This is the net force; an applied push must also account for any resistance.'))
answer(113,p('Choose the direction away from Rhys as positive. Gareth’s pull is positive and Rhys’s is negative: F<sub>Gareth</sub> − 24 = (1.25)(1.4) = 1.75 N.','F<sub>Gareth</sub> = <strong>25.75 N</strong> before rounding, about 26 N. The end pulls differ because the rope has mass and accelerates; each person-rope contact still obeys Newton’s third law.'))
answer(114,p('The scale measures the upward normal force N, not a change in the person’s gravitational weight.')+parts('At rest: N = mg = (70)(9.81) = <strong>686.7 N</strong>, about 687 N.','Accelerating upward: N − mg = ma, so N = m(g + a) = (70)(9.81 + 2.25) = <strong>844.2 N</strong>, about 844 N.')+p('At the subsequent constant speed, acceleration is zero and the reading returns to about 687 N. The 1.75 s duration affects the velocity change, not the normal force during constant acceleration.'))
answer(115,p('400 g = 0.400 kg. Choose the initial direction positive: a = −0.60/0.400 = −1.5 m/s².','t = (0 − 0.50)/(−1.5) = <strong>0.33 s</strong>.'))
answer(116,p('He moves <strong>away from the boards</strong>. His push on the boards is balanced by their equal and opposite push on him; it is the force on him that accelerates him.'))
answer(117,p('Assuming horizontal resistance is negligible, the boards exert 15 N on Owen. a = 15/40 = <strong>0.375 m/s²</strong> before rounding, about 0.38 m/s² away from the boards.'))
answer(118,p('Choose forward positive. F<sub>net</sub> = ma = (2100)(−4.5) = <strong>−9450 N</strong>, about −9.5 × 10³ N, opposite the car’s velocity.'))
answer(119,p('During upward acceleration, the supporting force must provide both weight support and acceleration: T − mg = ma, so T = m(g + a).','At the 275 N limit, m<sub>max</sub> = 275/(9.81 + 6.0) = <strong>17.4 kg</strong> before rounding, about 17 kg. This ignores the bag’s own mass and assumes its stated force limit applies to the lift.'))
answer(120,parts('Assuming a start from rest, 100 km/h = 27.78 m/s. a<sub>avg</sub> = 27.78/1.95 = <strong>14.2 m/s²</strong>. The vehicle and driver masses are not needed to calculate acceleration from the speed change and elapsed time.','Assuming a start from rest and constant acceleration for the 350 m run, d = ½at² gives a = 2(350)/(4.67)² = <strong>32.1 m/s²</strong>. Then v<sub>f</sub> = at = <strong>150 m/s</strong>, about 540 km/h.')+p('These are different acceleration estimates. One constant acceleration cannot satisfy both sets of data from the same rest start. Part (b) is a separate idealized constant-acceleration calculation.'))
answer(121,p('Choose the ball’s initial direction positive. a<sub>avg</sub> = (v<sub>f</sub> − v<sub>i</sub>)/Δt = (0 − 25)/0.05 = <strong>−500 m/s²</strong>, opposite the initial velocity. Mass is not needed for this acceleration calculation.'))

